---
/** 2026-10-01 cleanup round, item 2: rewritten so the sources list and the
 *  "how often" line are GENERATED from the actual per-town config
 *  (via lib/about-sources.ts's aboutSourcesFor(), the same "can't drift"
 *  machinery /about already uses) instead of a Brookings-only hand-written
 *  table with every other town falling back to a vague blurb -- the
 *  previous version leaked Brookings-shaped assumptions ("every hour")
 *  onto Moreno Valley and Broomfield, which don't share that cadence, and
 *  never enumerated Ticketmaster, Chamber/GrowthZone, or Worker Pulse's own
 *  sourcing at all.
 *
 *  Three facts below were explicitly verified against the real pipeline
 *  before writing this page, not assumed:
 *   1. No person reviews an item before it appears. Checked:
 *      ai_pipeline/publish.py's only "human review" reference (line ~699)
 *      is queuing an unresolved VENUE NAME STRING for a data-quality
 *      review queue (ai_pipeline/venue_registry.py) -- unrelated to content
 *      approval. There is no gate between generation and publish anywhere
 *      in the pipeline.
 *   2. The real cadence is every 6 hours, not hourly, for all three towns --
 *      confirmed via the "every 6 hours" cron in scrape.yml, moval-scrape.yml,
 *      and broomfield-scrape.yml alike (lowered from hourly 2026-08-28, per
 *      scrape.yml's own comment).
 *   3. /corrections exists, links from /contact, and honestly shows "No
 *      corrections have been logged yet" rather than being an empty stub.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { siteConfig } from '../lib/site-config';
import { aboutSourcesFor } from '../lib/about-sources';

const sources = aboutSourcesFor(siteConfig.townId);
---
<BaseLayout title={`How we gather this — ${siteConfig.siteName}`}
  description={`Every source ${siteConfig.siteName} draws from, how the AI-written pieces are checked, and what's reviewed by a person (nothing, before publish).`}>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>How we gather this</h1>
    <p>{siteConfig.siteName} doesn't have a newsroom. It has a set of public
    data sources, refreshed every 6 hours, and a language model that turns
    raw agendas, calendars, and public data into readable pages. No person
    reviews an individual item before it goes live — the checks below run
    automatically, every time. Here's exactly how that works.</p>

    <h2>The sources</h2>
    {sources.length > 0 ? (
      <ul class="sources">
        {sources.map((s) => (
          <li><a href={s.url} rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">{s.name}</a></li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    ) : (
      <p>{siteConfig.sourceBlurb}</p>
    )}
    <p>This list is generated from the same configuration the scrapers
    themselves run from — if a source is turned off, it drops off this page
    automatically, and nothing here is written by hand per town.</p>

    <h2>News, meetings, and listings</h2>
    <p>Meeting agendas, calendar listings, weather alerts, and similar
    factual items are summarized by Claude, an AI model made by Anthropic,
    working only from the source text — the actual agenda, the actual
    listing. It's instructed to use only facts present in that source and
    never to add names, numbers, or details that aren't there. A summary
    that appears to contradict its own source is rewritten or held back
    rather than published incomplete. Every one of these stories links back
    to its original source, so you can always read the full agenda or
    listing yourself.</p>

    <h2>Reviews, recipes, editorials, and columns</h2>
    <p>Some of what's on this site isn't a summary of one source document at
    all — film/TV reviews, recipes, editorials, and the science and
    quick-read columns are written by the same AI model, grounded in real
    facts (aggregate critic scores, a verified local venue, real civic
    data) but composed, not extracted. These are clearly AI-written and
    labeled as such on the page.</p>
    <p>Before anything in this group publishes, it has to pass a set of
    automatic checks: that it names the right town, state, and date; that
    it doesn't contradict its own source material or the facts already on
    this site; that editorials describe civic processes without endorsing a
    candidate or ballot measure, or naming a private business or person as
    a target of criticism; and, for reviews specifically, that it never
    claims a confirmed local showtime or "now playing" status this site has
    no actual data for. A draft that fails gets one rewrite attempt with the
    specific problem pointed out; if it still fails, nothing is published —
    there's no template fallback for a piece that fails these checks.</p>

    <h2>What we choose not to name</h2>
    <p>Meeting agendas sometimes include the names of private individuals —
    someone applying for a variance, say, or a permit. We refer to people by
    role instead ("an applicant in Preston Township") rather than by name.
    Organizations, businesses, and public officials acting in their official
    capacity are named normally.</p>

    <h2>Photos</h2>
    <p>Illustrative photos on category and topic pages come from Pexels,
    each individually credited to its photographer where it appears. They
    represent the general subject, not a specific event pictured in the
    story next to them.</p>

    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>If a summary gets something wrong, <a href="/contact/">tell us</a> and
    we'll fix it — usually within one refresh cycle, since the site rebuilds
    automatically. Logged corrections are public: see the
    <a href="/corrections/">corrections page</a>.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
.prose { max-width: 40rem; }
.prose h2 { margin-top: 2.25rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }
.prose p { margin: 0 0 1rem; }

.sources { margin: 0 0 1rem; padding-left: 1.25rem; display: grid; gap: 0.375rem; }
.sources li { font-size: 0.9375rem; }
</style>
